{
 "format": "lextor/1",
 "meta": {
  "title": "Un día en la ciudad",
  "text_lang": "es",
  "notes_lang": "en",
  "summary": "A beginner-friendly text about a typical day exploring a Spanish city. Covers daily routines, directions, and common interactions.",
  "created_at": "2026-01-20T00:00:00"
 },
 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "sentences": [
    {
     "text": "Un día en la ciudad",
     "translation": "A day in the city",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Un día en la ciudad",
       "gloss": "A day in the city",
       "note": "'Un' = a/an (masculine). 'Día' = day — one of the few Spanish words ending in '-a' that is masculine ('el día'). 'Ciudad' = city."
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "text": "Me despierto temprano por la mañana.",
     "translation": "I wake up early in the morning.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Me despierto temprano por la mañana.",
       "gloss": "I wake up early in the morning.",
       "note": "'Me despierto' = I wake up (reflexive verb 'despertarse'). Many daily routines use reflexive verbs in Spanish. 'Temprano' = early. 'Por la mañana' = in the morning."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Me ducho, me visto y bajo a la calle para desayunar en un café.",
     "translation": "I shower, I get dressed and I go down to the street to have breakfast at a café.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Me ducho, me visto",
       "gloss": "I shower, I get dressed",
       "note": "'Me ducho' = I shower (from 'ducharse'). 'Me visto' = I get dressed (from 'vestirse'). Both reflexive. In Spanish, you 'shower yourself' and 'dress yourself'."
      },
      {
       "text": "y bajo a la calle para desayunar en un café.",
       "gloss": "and I go down to the street to have breakfast at a café.",
       "note": "'Bajo' = I go down (from 'bajar'). 'La calle' = the street. 'Para' = in order to. 'Desayunar' = to have breakfast. In Spain, it's common to have breakfast at a café ('cafetería')."
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "text": "Pido un café con leche y una tostada con tomate.",
     "translation": "I order a coffee with milk and toast with tomato.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Pido un café con leche y una tostada con tomate.",
       "gloss": "I order a coffee with milk and toast with tomato.",
       "note": "'Pido' = I order/ask for (from 'pedir', an e→i stem-changing verb). 'Café con leche' = coffee with milk (the classic Spanish breakfast drink). 'Tostada con tomate' = a very typical Andalusian/Spanish breakfast."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "El camarero es muy amable y me dice «¡buen provecho!».",
     "translation": "The waiter is very friendly and says to me 'enjoy your meal!'.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "El camarero es muy amable",
       "gloss": "The waiter is very friendly",
       "note": "'Camarero' = waiter (female: 'camarera'). 'Amable' = friendly/kind. Note: 'amable' doesn't change for gender — it ends in '-e', not '-o/-a'."
      },
      {
       "text": "y me dice «¡buen provecho!».",
       "gloss": "and says to me 'enjoy your meal!'.",
       "note": "'Me dice' = says to me. 'Buen provecho' = enjoy your meal (lit. 'good profit/benefit'). Equivalent to French 'bon appétit'. Very commonly said in Spanish-speaking countries."
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "text": "Después del desayuno, camino por el centro de la ciudad.",
     "translation": "After breakfast, I walk through the city center.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Después del desayuno, camino por el centro de la ciudad.",
       "gloss": "After breakfast, I walk through the city center.",
       "note": "'Después del' = after the (contraction: de + el = del). 'Camino' = I walk (from 'caminar'). 'Por' = through/around. 'El centro' = the center/downtown."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Las calles están llenas de gente y hay músicos tocando en las esquinas.",
     "translation": "The streets are full of people and there are musicians playing on the corners.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Las calles están llenas de gente",
       "gloss": "The streets are full of people",
       "note": "'Las calles' = the streets (feminine plural). 'Están llenas' = are full ('estar' for temporary state, 'llenas' = full, fem. plural). 'Gente' = people (always singular in Spanish: 'la gente es')."
      },
      {
       "text": "y hay músicos tocando en las esquinas.",
       "gloss": "and there are musicians playing on the corners.",
       "note": "'Hay' = there is/there are (invariable — same form for singular and plural!). 'Músicos' = musicians. 'Tocando' = playing (present participle of 'tocar'). 'Esquinas' = corners."
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "text": "Le pregunto a una señora: «Disculpe, ¿dónde está el museo?».",
     "translation": "I ask a lady: 'Excuse me, where is the museum?'.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Le pregunto a una señora:",
       "gloss": "I ask a lady:",
       "note": "'Le pregunto a' = I ask (to). In Spanish, you need both 'le' (indirect object pronoun) AND 'a una señora' — this redundancy is normal. 'Señora' = lady/ma'am."
      },
      {
       "text": "«Disculpe, ¿dónde está el museo?».",
       "gloss": "'Excuse me, where is the museum?'.",
       "note": "'Disculpe' = excuse me (formal, from 'disculpar' in usted form). '¿Dónde está?' = where is? The accent on 'dónde' shows it's a question word. 'Museo' = museum (cognate!)."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Ella me responde con una sonrisa: «Está muy cerca, todo recto y luego a la derecha».",
     "translation": "She answers me with a smile: 'It's very close, straight ahead and then to the right'.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Ella me responde con una sonrisa:",
       "gloss": "She answers me with a smile:",
       "note": "'Ella' = she. 'Me responde' = answers me. 'Con una sonrisa' = with a smile. 'Sonrisa' comes from 'sonreír' (to smile, lit. 'to sub-laugh')."
      },
      {
       "text": "«Está muy cerca, todo recto y luego a la derecha».",
       "gloss": "'It's very close, straight ahead and then to the right'.",
       "note": "'Muy cerca' = very close. 'Todo recto' = straight ahead (in Latin America: 'derecho' or 'de frente'). 'Luego' = then. 'A la derecha' = to the right."
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "text": "Al final del día, estoy cansado pero feliz.",
     "translation": "At the end of the day, I'm tired but happy.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Al final del día, estoy cansado pero feliz.",
       "gloss": "At the end of the day, I'm tired but happy.",
       "note": "'Al final del día' = at the end of the day. 'Estoy cansado' uses 'estar' (not 'ser') because tiredness is a temporary state. 'Pero' = but. 'Feliz' = happy."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Esta ciudad es maravillosa.",
     "translation": "This city is wonderful.",
     "chunks": [
      {
       "text": "Esta ciudad es maravillosa.",
       "gloss": "This city is wonderful.",
       "note": "'Esta' = this (feminine, for 'ciudad'). 'Es maravillosa' uses 'ser' because it describes an inherent quality. 'Maravillosa' = wonderful/marvelous."
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}